window.SAQ_PAPER_QUESTIONS["2024-2-st-george"]=[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"Your triage nurse asks you to have a look at a rash. A 35-year-old male has presented with a 3-day history of fever, lethargy and myalgias. The rash started on his face and has now spread to his trunk, palms and soles. You are concerned this might be a Monkeypox infection.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State (2) clinical or epidemiological risk factors would you seek on screening history that would lend weight to your suspicions. (2 marks) 1. 2."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Give (4) important INFECTIOUS differentials for the above rash and state a distinguishing clinical feature for each condition. (4 marks) Differential diagnosis Distinguishing feature"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline (3) important aspects of managing this patient you strongly suspect of having Monkeypox. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3. You explain the likely diagnosis to your patient. He says that his partner is returning from a 2- week interstate work trip tomorrow and he is concerned about spreading the infection to them."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State (2) specific pieces of advice you would give to address his concerns. (2 marks) 1. 2."}],"propPages":["asset:img41a716f804fa11675a"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Other typical symptoms : progression of rash from macules -&gt; papules -&gt; vesicles -&gt;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pustules (synchronous development), tender lymphadenopathy, chills and sweats,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">headache, may involve genitals/anus/mouth</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Close contact with conﬁrmed case of Mpox</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Recent overseas travel to endemic areas eg. Central and West Africa</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• MSM/bisexual male</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>• <strong>Chicken pox (varicella zoster)</strong> – pustules/vesicles/scabs present at the same time</td><td>(lesions at diﬀerent stages)</td></tr><tr><td>• <strong>Hand-foot-mouth (coxsackie)</strong> – rash usually limited to hands, feet and mouth, small</td><td>ovoid grey pustules</td></tr><tr><td>• Secondary syphilis (treponema pallidum) – maculopapular rash palms and soles, may</td><td>be associated alopecia, condylomata lata, mucous patches</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Infection control : isolate in single room, minimum contact + droplet precautions </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Involve local infectious disease/microbiology team +/- notify Public Health </strong><em>(should </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>advise on sample collection for PCR testing) </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Seek and treat complication such eg. secondary bacterial skin infection, pneumonia,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">sepsis, encephalitis, ocular complications</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Manage symptoms eg. pain relief, dehydration</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Patient should isolate until the rash is fully resolved ie. lesions have crusted and the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">scabs have fallen oﬀ</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Partner should avoid contact with bedding and personal care items of the patient and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">practise good hand hygiene</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Partner may be eligible for free vaccination</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"A 48-year-old man with a background of alcoholic cirrhosis is brought in by ambulance with massive haematemesis. You arrive just as your team are applying monitoring and securing large bore IV access in the resus bay. The patient is pale and lethargic with the following vital signs :\n\nHR\n120\n\nBP\n78/40\n\nRR\n\nSats\n88% RA\n\nGCS\n\nTemp\n35.5°C","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Outline your strategy for haemodynamic resuscitation prior to intubation. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State (6) strategies for minimising airway soiling and aspiration during RSI. (6 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6. The patient is successfully intubated."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List (3) pharmacological treatments you would prescribe in the context of UGI bleed with a rationale for each. (3 marks) Treatment Rationale"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Warmed O group blood </strong>and activation of MTP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Controlled hypotension</strong> : aim SBP 90-100mmHg (‘perfusing BP’) to prevent further</div><div class=\"answer-line\">bleed</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Judicious use of vasopressor eg. NAd infusion titrated to desired MAP</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em><strong>NOT</strong></em><strong> </strong><em><strong>TXA given results of HALT-IT Trial </strong></em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Head-up positioning 45 deg (or LLP) during preO2 and intubation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Prokinetic eg metoclopramide 20mg IV or erythromycin 250mg IV over 10 minutes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Careful large bore NGT insertion</strong> and aspiration of gastric contents</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Double suction set-up</strong> with large bore catheter eg Ducanto if available, dedicated</div><div class=\"answer-line\">assistant</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Avoid PPV</strong> to prevent gastric insuﬄation, if BVM necessary use slow, gentle technique</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• SALAD Technique with CMAC videolaryngoscope – can use as DL if camera soiled</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. <strong>Ceftriaxone 1g</strong> – has been shown to reduce mortality, bacterial infection, rebleed and</td><td>hospital LOS in cirrhotic patients.</td></tr><tr><td>2. Octreotide 50 microg bolus then 50microg/hr infusion – reduces splanchnic blood ﬂow</td><td>and acid production, may prevent rebleed in variceal and nonvariceal bleed</td></tr><tr><td>3. Pantoprazole 80mg IV bolus – little evidence to support use, however, may reduce high</td><td>risk stigmata of bleeding at time of scope</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"An 11-year-old boy is brought into your emergency department after his pelvis and lower\nabdomen were driven over by a semi-trailer. An X-ray is performed :","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (4) relevant findings on the X-ray. (2 marks -1/2 mark each) 1. 2. 3. 4."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (5) complications of this injury. (5 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline (5) steps in the management of the suspected injuries associated with this X- ray. (5 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5."}],"propPages":["asset:imga169dbe5305ef3347b"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong>Paediatric AP pelvic Xray with multiple developmental physes – may have SH1</div><div class=\"answer-line\">fractures in these areas.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong>Comminuted right superior and inferior pubic ramus fractures</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong>Linear fracture fragment adjacent to ischium/ilium ?avulsion</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>4. </strong>Vertical distraction injury left hemi-pelvis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong>UGT – urethral – partial/complete disruption</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong>UGT – bladder – intra/extraperitoneal</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong>Bleeding – fracture ends, venous and arterial (likely with vertical distraction injury)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>4. </strong>Nerve injury – nerves supplied by roots L4/5 – S5 – sciatic etc</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>5. </strong>Bowel – need to consider penetrating injury from bone or hollow viscus rupture</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Would also consider complications of fractures – nonunion, malalignment, OM etc</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong><strong>Stop bleeding</strong> – minimise pelvic movement, reduce pelvic fracture – traction on LLL</div><div class=\"answer-line\">until ankle aligned then secure followed by pelvic binding and XR confirmation of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">adequate reduction.  Consider angiography + angioembolisation or pelvic packing if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">unstable</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong><strong>Replace blood and factors</strong> – prevent fibrinolysis (TXA 10mg-15mg/kg IV bolus if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">within 3h of injury).  Replace blood product loss dictated by Hb, haemodynamic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">stability, ongoing bleeding, likely coagulopathy.  MTP as indicated.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong><strong>Analgesia </strong>– IV fentanyl + ketamine preferred as defend BP better than morphine,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">avoid NSAIDs due to antiplatelet effects</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>4. </strong><strong>Establish UO</strong> – if possible urethral injury, consider single attempt at catheter vs on</div><div class=\"answer-line\">table urethrogram (only if angioembolisation completed or not occurring).  May need</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SPC, US-guided, beware retroperitoneal haematoma mimicking bladder</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>5. </strong><strong>Ensure no blood on PR exam</strong> – if present, suggests bowel contamination of pelvis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">possible.   Need bowel isolation procedure and pelvic washout + amp/gent/flag</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"A 72-year-old gentleman presents to ED with increasing low back pain over the past 6 weeks. This pain is relentless, causing sleepless nights. The patient had previously presented to hospital and was discharged with a diagnosis of a muscle strain. He has not improved with anti-inflammatories and physiotherapy. He also complains of feeling generally fatigued. Vital signs today are :\n\nHR\n\nBP\n126/85\n\nRR\n\nSats\n97% RA\n\nTemp\n36 ̊C Initial blood test results are as follows :\n\nNa\n141\n\nK\n4.1\n\nCl\n103\n\nHCO3\n\nUrea\n12.4\n\nCreatinine\n204\nBaseline 85\nCalcium Corr 3.45","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Given this clinical picture, what historical features will you explore to confirm your diagnosis? (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List and justify (3) SPECIFIC investigations that can help confirm your diagnosis. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State your management priorities for this patient in the emergency department. (5 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Constitutional symptoms: lethargy, night sweats, fever, weight loss, anorexia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Neurological symptoms: related to spinal cord compression, autonomic dysfunction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(urinary retention), association with carpel tunnel syndrome</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• History of pathological fractures</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Recurrent infections – association with pneumococcus, shingles</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Related to anaemia – dyspnoea, weakness, dizziness, chest pain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Related to hyperviscosity – blurred vision, headaches, dizziness, neuro deﬁcit</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Related to hypercalcaemia: confusion, somnolence, constipation</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• FBC – anaemia and thrombocytopaenia due to marrow inﬁltration</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• CT skeletal survey – lytic lesions esp skull, ribs, spine and long bones</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Serum electrophoresis – detects and quantiﬁes amount of monoclonal Ig</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• 24-hour urine protein electrophoresis - monoclonal antibodies/BJP</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• (UA for proteinuria ½ mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Bone marrow aspirate and trephine</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Pain management</strong> : ideally opioid with reasonable dose and route, avoidance of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">NSAIDs due to renal impairment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Management of hypercalcaemia</strong> : aggressive IV rehydration with 0.9% saline to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">euvolaemia then aiming UO 1-2mL/kg/hr, IV bisphosphonates +/- calcitonin +/-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">corticosteroids</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Management of renal failure : avoid nephrotoxins, IVF</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Blood transfusion for symptomatic anaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Early consultation with haematology, nephrology +/ neurosurgery/orthopaedics if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">signiﬁcant spinal cord/bony injury</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"An 83-year-old female, from a non-English speaking background, is brought in with increasing right thigh pain and swelling after falling out of bed 3 days prior. Her son is caring for her full- time at home with no additional help. He states she is not currently taking any medications, she is usually confused but can walk with assistance. On examination she is curled up in foetal position and moaning in pain. X-rays of her pelvis and femur are shown below :","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Outline your approach to managing this patient. (6 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (4) individual and (2) perpetrator risk factors for elder abuse. (6 marks) Individual risk factors 1. 2. 3. 4. Perpetrator risk factors 1. 2."}],"propPages":["asset:img1a173e4b1f127a8286","asset:imgddb662ddcf3a125c0e"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Early appropriate analgesia titrated to comfort (paracetamol + PO/IV fentanyl + FIB</div><div class=\"answer-line\">once able to position appropriately)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Seek and treat important complications</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Signiﬁcant blood loss -&gt; PRBC transfusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Compartment syndrome/neurovascular compromise -&gt; urgent ortho</div><div class=\"answer-line\">involvement if concerns</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Fracture reduction under procedural sedation and application of traction splint</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Discuss ceiling of care with NOK and document in notes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Refer to orthopaedics/orthogeriatric team for admission, involve SW re ?elder</div><div class=\"answer-line\">abuse/neglect</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Supportive care : IDC, pressure area care, seek and treat other injuries</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">neglect/elder-abuse#heading1</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"Questions 6 (12 marks)\nA 4-year-old boy is brought to ED by his mum with ataxia and this :","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Give (2) potentially life-threatening complications that might arise from this clinical scenario. (2 marks) 1. 2."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (3) other potential complications. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Give (4) important differentials of acute ataxia in this age group. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your steps for removal. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."}],"propPages":["asset:img89e9909f029bd52338"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Respiratory failure secondary to respiratory muscle paralysis (ascending)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Anaphylaxis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Local reaction (can be prolonged if head left in situ after removal)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Systemic infections : Rickettsia, Q fever (Coxiella burnetti)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Mammalian meat allergy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• (DSCATT = debilitating symptom complexes associated to ticks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>No evidence of Lyme disease being transmitted by ticks in Australia </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Post infectious (acute cerebellar ataxia)</strong> – varicella, mycoplasma, EBV, HHV6,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">parvovirus B19, enterovirus, post-immunisation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Tumours – neuroblastoma </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Toxins – antihistamines, ETOH and toxic alcohols, anticholinergics…</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Immune (eg. ADEM)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Other (1 of) : metabolic, infections, trauma</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Ensure tick is killed prior to removal by freezing or application of permethrin cream</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Grab the tick with forcep tips as close to the skin as possible</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Pull gently to ensure tick remains intact on removal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Resources :</div><div class=\"answer-line\">australia_0.pdf</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"A 36-year-old man presents after a syncopal episode during a game of tennis. He has no\nknown past medical history. His ECG is shown below :","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (2) relevant findings seen above. (2 marks) 1. 2. You go on to do a bedside cardiac ultrasound. A parasternal long axis, and parasternal short axis view is shown below."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is the finding shown by the arrows on these images? (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What SPECIFIC sign would you assess for on examination? (1 mark)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Give (2) most common causes of presyncope/syncope related to this condition. (2 marks) 1. 2."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List (3) additional complications of this condition. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."},{"id":"f","label":"Part F","prompt":"List and justify (2) specific investigations for this condition. (2 marks)"},{"id":"g","label":"Part G","prompt":"What key piece of advice would you provide? (1 mark)"}],"propPages":["asset:imge409f72925ecc5cb18","asset:img8579377d3ef7ca5859"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Sinus rhythm (HCM often associated with AF)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Minor scooped ST elevation inferior leads</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Asymmetrical septal wall thickening/hypertrophy (ASH)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Crescendo systolic murmur increased with Valsalva/squat to stand -&gt; increases LVOT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">obstruction</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• LVOT obstruction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Dysrhythmia esp AF, VF/VT</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Heart failure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Mitral valve dysfunction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Ischaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Stroke secondary to AF</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Infective endocarditis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part F</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Formal TTE – assess for ASH/SAM/apical thickening/LA dilatation/MR</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• 24-48 hour Holter monitor – assess for dysrhythmias</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Cardiac MRI – detailed imaging of myocardium (useful in detecting ﬁbrosis which</div><div class=\"answer-line\">increases risk of arrhythmias and SCD)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Exercise stress test – assess functional capacity/risk stratiﬁcation prior to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">commencement of treatment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Genetic testing – conﬁrmation of diagnosis/familial screening as autosomal dominant</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part G</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Needs early cardiology review and to avoid strenuous exercise until cleared by</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cardiologist as at risk of sudden-cardiac death</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"A 32-year-old woman with a history of Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome presents to your ED with a 1- day history of atraumatic headache and left sided neck pain. Vitals are all within normal limits except for BP 160/90.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Other than vertebral artery dissection, list (4) possible differentials of headache in this patient with (2) associated clinical features. (8 marks) Differential diagnosis (1 mark)\nClinical features x 2 (1/2 mark each)\nCT angiogram demonstrates acute left vertebral artery dissection with evidence of cerebral infarction."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (4) examination findings you would expect in a posterior circulation stroke syndrome. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. On advising the patient of her diagnosis, she becomes angry and distressed as she presented\nwith similar symptoms 3 days ago and was discharged home with a diagnosis of migraine. At\nthe time, she felt dismissed by the staff members involved in her care."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline (6) important steps in your immediate response to the patient’s complaint. (6 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Carotid artery dissection</td><td>Unilateral facial pain, unilateral headache, partial</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Horner’s syndrome – miosis and ptosis</td></tr><tr><td>Subarachnoid haemorrhage</td><td>Thunderclap/worst ever headache, meningism,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">photophobia</td></tr><tr><td>Migraine</td><td>Aura, phono/photophobia, unilateral, throbbing</td></tr><tr><td>Space occupying lesion</td><td>Signs of raised ICP ie. papilloedema, vomiting,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">headache worse with position</td></tr><tr><td>CVST</td><td>Focal neuro deficit, visual disturbance, seizures,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">AMS</td></tr><tr><td>Posterior reversible encephalopathy</td><td>Seizures, disturbed vision, headache, and altered</td></tr><tr><td>syndrome (PRES)</td><td>mental state</td></tr><tr><td>Infection – encephalitis, meningitis</td><td>Fever, AMS, signs of meningism</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Tintinali, Dunn</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Unilateral limb weakness/ataxia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Oropharyngeal dysfunction (bulbar muscle), dysarthria, dysphagia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Contralateral homonymous hemianopia/ blurred vision</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. HINTS exam consistent with central lesion (neg HI, bidirectional/vertical nystagmus,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pos test of skew)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5. Hearing loss</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tintinali</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Uptodate - <strong>Cerebral and cervical artery dissection: Clinical features and diagnosis</strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">•<strong> </strong>Apology and open disclosure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•<strong> </strong>Listen to the patient and ensure they feel heard (oﬀer complaints/patient advocate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">contact information)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•<strong> </strong>Address acute management issues and provide supportive and symptomatic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">management</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•<strong> </strong>Advise that the incident will be investigated and advise on next steps with a set</div><div class=\"answer-line\">timeframe</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•<strong> </strong>Notify management and enter complaint into incident management system</div><div class=\"answer-line\">•<strong> </strong>Document discussion and plan clearly in the notes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dunn, litfl https://litfl.com/osce-patient-complaint/</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"You are looking after a 10-week-old male infant who presented febrile and listless.\nUrgent bloods results have been called through from the lab : Reference Range Sodium 133 135-145 mmol/L Potassium 8.2 3.5-5.2 mmol/L Chloride 104 95-110 mmol/L Calcium Ionised 0.84 0.95-1.50 mmol/L Blood Glucose 7.2 3.9-6.1 mmol/L Blood Lactate 3.8 0.2-2.0 mmol/L\n\nWCC\n344.13 6.5-17.5 x10^9/L\n\nHb\n95-135 g/L PLT 150-450 x10^9/L HCT 0.178 0.29-0.41 L/L MCV 104.7 72-82 fL RCC 1.70 4.5-6.5 x10^12/L","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Provide an explanation for (5) abnormalities (other than K+) in these results. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List a possible complication related to (4) of the abnormal blood results (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What is the most likely diagnosis? (1 mark) An ECG is performed :"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List (4) normal ECG parameter differences between an infant and adult ECG (4 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Outline your approach to his K+ result. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."}],"propPages":["asset:img62a3d76c52f73dfb25"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>WCC 344</td><td>Acute Leukaemia</td></tr><tr><td>Hb 64</td><td>Marrow invasion by blasts = decreased production.</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Physiological +/-infective haemolysis</td></tr><tr><td>Plt 33</td><td>Marrow invasion by blasts = decreased production. ?</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Splenomegaly with sequestration</td></tr><tr><td>Lactate 3.8</td><td>Septic hypoperfusion, diminished oxygen carrying capacity as</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">anaemic</td></tr><tr><td>Ca 0.84</td><td>Possible tumour lysis causing phosphate release and Ca++</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">precipitation</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>WCC 344</td><td>Tumour lysis, hyperviscosity syndrome</td></tr><tr><td>Hb 64</td><td>Poor oxygen carrying capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Plt 33</td><td>Petechiae, bleeding</td></tr><tr><td>K+ 8.2</td><td>Cardiac arrhythmia, arrest</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute Lymphocytic Leukaemia</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>HR 120-160</td><td>HR 60-80</td></tr><tr><td>Right Ventricular dominance R waves V1-V6</td><td>LV Dominance – early R wave progression to</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">dominant S</td></tr><tr><td>Right axis (up to 180 degrees)</td><td>Axis 0-90</td></tr><tr><td>PR, QRS max shorter than adult max</td><td>PR, QRS max 0.2 and 0.12 respectively</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>K+ 8.2 – ? tumour lysis ? pseudohyperkalaemia (NB white cell haemolysis doesn’t produce </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>haem pigment so not picked up by spectrophotometry) </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>ECG not c/w hyperkalaemia – explanation – slow rise of K+ or in vitro </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>acquisition/centrifugation haemolysis </em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>1. </strong></td><td>Continue to monitor ECG for hyperkalaemic changes as child at risk of tumour lysis</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">and hyperK<strong> </strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. </strong></td><td>Minimise acquisition/centrifugation haemolysis with free ﬂowing VBG</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">If VBG K+ &lt; 5.5 – in vitro haemolysis, no acute Rx</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. </strong></td><td>If VBG K+ &gt; 5.5, normal ECG – Rx with K+ shift – rehydrate, insulin/dextrose/</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">NaHCO3</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4. </strong></td><td>If VBG K+ &gt; 5.5 and ECG changes – as above, but consider ca gluconate – relative CI</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">with tumour lysis</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false,"investigationTables":[{"id":"investigation-1","title":"Blood results","headers":["Test","Result","Reference Range"],"rows":[["Sodium","133","135-145 mmol/L"],["Potassium","8.2","3.5-5.2 mmol/L"],["Chloride","104","95-110 mmol/L"],["Calcium Ionised","0.84","0.95-1.50 mmol/L"],["Blood Glucose","7.2","3.9-6.1 mmol/L"],["Blood Lactate","3.8","0.2-2.0 mmol/L"],["WCC","344.13","6.5-17.5 x10^9/L"],["Hb","64","95-135 g/L"],["PLT","33","150-450 x10^9/L"],["HCT","0.178","0.29-0.41 L/L"],["MCV","104.7","72-82 fL"],["RCC","1.70","4.5-6.5 x10^12/L"]]}]},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"A 38-year-old lady, G1P0 at 34/40 gestation, is brought in by ambulance to your tertiary\nemergency department in cardiac arrest","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (4) causes of cardiac arrest you need to specifically consider in the pregnant patient. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. You and your team continue the ALS protocol."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"In the table below, list (4) issues which complicate the management of the arrested pregnant patient and provide a solution for each. (4 marks) Issue Solution"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What are the indications for resuscitative hysterotomy? (2 mark) 1. 2."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"State (2) benefits of the procedure. (2 marks) 1. 2."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Massive PE</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Haemorrhage from uterine rupture/placental abruption/praevia, liver rupture or DIC</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Sepsis eg. Chorioamnionitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Maternal cardiac disease – peripartum cardiomyopathy, dysrhythmia, aortic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">dissection, MI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy eg. HELLP/eclampsia/preeclampsia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Amniotic ﬂuid embolism</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Poisoning and self-harm</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>Management</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diﬃcult airway (oedema, friability)</td><td>Most experienced operator, careful intubation with</td></tr><tr><td>smaller ETT</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Limited oxygen reserve</td><td>Early ventilatory support with FIO2 1.0</td></tr><tr><td>High risk of aspiration</td><td>Suction readily available, secure airway early</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Aortocaval compression</strong></td><td><strong>Continuous left lateral uterine displacement </strong></td></tr><tr><td>Resuscitative hysterotomy (mentioned in subquestion)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Diﬃculty placing deﬁb pads due to</td><td>Biaxillary or AP pad placement</td></tr><tr><td>enlarged breast tissue</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Two patients</td><td>Early notiﬁcation of obstetric and paediatric teams</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Cardiac arrest in patient &gt;20 weeks gestation (fundus above umbilicus)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Maternal cardiac arrest without ROSC within 4 minutes</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Reduces aortocaval compression, improves preload</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Improves maternal pulmonary mechanics</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Reduces maternal oxygen demand</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• In the presence of truly unsalvageable maternal pathology, oﬀers a chance for foetal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">survival if &gt;24/40</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"A 48-year-old woman presents with a 4-day history of a sore throat and progressive difficulty in swallowing. You note that she appears unwell, has stridor at rest and is finding it very painful to swallow her secretions. Her initial observations are : Temp 38.6, HR 118bpm, BP 110/80, RR 24, Sats 95% RA","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (3) differential diagnoses for this presentation. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3. A lateral neck X-ray is obtained :"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the (2) MAIN abnormalities in the above radiograph. (2 mark) 1. 2."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State key steps in your immediate management of this patient. (7 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6. 7."}],"propPages":["asset:imgc833633647f6df0b47"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Epiglottitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bacterial tracheitis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tonsillitis/peritonsillar abscess</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Others </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Marked widening of the pre-vertebral soft tissue between C2-C7 with multiple gas foci</div><div class=\"answer-line\">within the swelling at the level C3-C4.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Position – keep patient sitting in a comfortable position and keep them calm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Difficult airway kit checked and immediately available at the bedside with team roles</div><div class=\"answer-line\">allocated, team briefed, including possible need for FONA (may be difficult due to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">distorted anatomy)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Urgent ENT and anaesthetic review -&gt;&gt; ideally AFOI in OT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Nebulised adrenaline 5mg 1:1000, repeated as needed</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Steroids – Dexamethasone 8-12mg IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Antibiotic therapy (eTG guidelines) – Amoxicillin + Clavulanate 1g/0.2g IV q 6hr or</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cefazolin 2g q 8hr IV + Metronidazole 500mg q12hr IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Analgesia – eg Fentanyl 25mcg IV aliquots titrated to clinical response/respiratory</div><div class=\"answer-line\">rate/level of consciousness (or appropriate dose Morphine or other)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Supportive care eg IV crystalloid fluid to treat dehydration, analgesia</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"You are working in a tertiary hospital when a BAT phone is received from an ambulance on its way from the airport. Flanked by the AFP, a notable Russian dissident has suddenly become unwell on a flight that was leaving Sydney en route to London. The plane turned around within 30 minutes of take-off. He appears to have vomited and has been both incontinent of bladder and bowels. Vitals are as follows:\n\nHR\n\nBP\n85/50\n\nRR\n28 (shallow breathing)\n\nSats\n92% 15L/min NRB\n\nGCS\n10 (E3V3M4), agitated, pinpoint pupils","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Outline (2) important aspects of your IMMEDIATE management. (4 marks) 1. 2."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State (4) immediate or anticipated life-threatening complications of this poisoning and actions you would take to mitigate. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Give (2) SPECIFIC antidotes, their dose and mechanism of action. (4 marks) Antidote (with dose) Mechanism of action"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Personal protection</strong>: non-rebreathing apparatus and sealed suits if pt not</div><div class=\"answer-line\">decontaminated at the airport otherwise PPE, negative pressure room, educate team</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Decontamination </strong>: all clothing removed and securely packaged for AFP, use towels to</div><div class=\"answer-line\">dab dry secretions, wash skin thoroughly with soap and water, notify HazMat</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Respiratory failure secondary to excess secretions/bronchospasm/muscle paralysis –</div><div class=\"answer-line\">atropine (below), if no significant improvement will require RSI and mechanical</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ventilation, NDMR rather than sux</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Haemodynamic instability – atropine, IV fluid boluses, art line insertion and early</div><div class=\"answer-line\">vasopressor support</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Arrhythmias – continuous ECG monitoring, correction of electrolyte imbalance esp K</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and Mg from GI losses</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Seizures secondary to CNS excitoxicity – IV BZD eg, midaz/diaz and appropriate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">sedation post intubation</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Atropine 1-3mg IV, doubling dose q5-10 min</strong> : blocks ACh receptors (muscarinic &gt;</div><div class=\"answer-line\">nicotinic).  Endpoint is clearing of bronchial secretions.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Pralidoxime 30mg/kg IV over 15-30 min-&gt; 8mg/kg/hr (must be administered</div><div class=\"answer-line\">within minutes to few hours before ‘ageing’ of enzyme-nerve agent bond becomes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">irreversible) : reactivates acetylcholinesterase at nicotinic receptors</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"You are the consultant on call at a mixed tertiary centre and receive a BATPHONE regarding a 2-year-old boy with ongoing generalised seizures for > 15 minutes despite 2 appropriate doses\n• f IV midazolam and is requiring BVM to assist ventilation.\nHe had fevers & one vomit since morning, no significant background medical history. Weight\nis known to be 15kg. The patient is expected to arrive in under 10 minutes.\nResus nursing staff ask what medications you would like prepared for patient arrival.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State (6) intravenous therapies with dose you anticipate are likely to be required in the acute management of this patient, including (3) unrelated to airway management. (6 marks) Medication Dose You successfully intubate the child, confirm end-tidal CO2 & bilateral lung sliding on ultrasound however are having difficulty ventilating following intubation. O2 sats remain at 92% on 100% FiO2 and tidal volumes are 1⁄2 of predicted The following CXR & ABG is available soon after intubation:"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State (6) interventions to improve ventilation in this child given the above results. (6 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5. 6."}],"propPages":["asset:img13ef92c8afacd97650","asset:img8d0d4bde0e544fbc56"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Ketamine OR</td><td>2mg/ kg</td></tr><tr><td>Propofol (better)</td><td>2mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Rocuronium / sux</td><td>2mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Atropine</td><td>0.02mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Levetiracetam</td><td>20-40mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Phenytoin</td><td>20-40mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Cefotaxime / Ceftriaxone</td><td>50mg /kg</td></tr><tr><td>Ampicillin</td><td>50mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Dextrose 10%</td><td>2mL/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Normal saline 0.9%</td><td>10-20mg/kg bolus</td></tr><tr><td>Dexamethasone</td><td>0.3-0.6mg/kg</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Insert NG tube </strong> - gastric decompression</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Minimise deadspace in ventilation circuit</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Positioning - sit upright at 30 degrees</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Increase respiratory rate eg from 30 to 35</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Increase PEEP to max 10cmH20</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Increase sedation / bolus eg midazolam / morph / ketamine (reduce dyssynchrony)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Re-paralyse with suxamethonium / rocuronium (also reduce dyssynchrony however</div><div class=\"answer-line\">should qualify diﬃcult to detect ongoing seizure activity)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"A 5-year-old boy is brought in by his mother with progressive left eye swelling, pain and\nnausea.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (4) relevant clinical examination findings that would help differentiate orbital and periorbital cellulitis (4 marks) Orbital Periorbital"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (2) other differentials you should consider (2 marks) 1. 2. Your examination findings are consistent with orbital cellulitis."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State (3) priorities in his management including drug dose/route where relevant. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List (3) significant complications of orbital cellulitis. (3 marks) 1. 2."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Child can appear toxic with high-grade fever</td><td>Usually low-grade fever</td></tr><tr><td>RAPD</td><td>No RAPD</td></tr><tr><td>Visual acuity loss</td><td>Normal vision</td></tr><tr><td>Proptosis</td><td>Nil</td></tr><tr><td>Painful EOM movement</td><td>Non painful movements</td></tr><tr><td>Ophthalmoplegia / diplopia</td><td>No diplopia</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Trauma</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Allergy/sting/bite</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong><strong>Early antibiotics : </strong>IV cefotaxime 50mg/kg q8H or ceftriaxone 50mg/kg daily AND</div><div class=\"answer-line\">flucloxacillin 50mg/kg q6H</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong><strong>Analgesia</strong> : paracetamol 15mg/kg + ibuprofen 10mg/kg +/- oxycodone 0.05mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Antiemetic : </strong>ondansetron 4mg PO TDS (estimated weight 15kg based on age)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong><strong>Urgent Ophthalmology input</strong> + CT/MRI to look for collection and aid surgical planning</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong>Optic nerve compression / neuropathy -&gt; <strong>vision loss</strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong>Cavernous sinus thrombosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3 </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Meningitis/cerebral abscess</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Osteomyelitis/orbital abscess</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"A 75-year-old female presents to ED with right loin pain and rigors. She has a past history of T2DM and hypertension and takes regular metformin, gliclazide, ramipril and amlodipine.\nHer vital signs are:\n\nHR\n102\n\nBP\n90/60\n\nRR\n\nSaO2\n95% RA\n\nTemp\n39°C Her CT KUB is shown below :","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List (4) abnormalities visible on this CT scan (4 marks) and provide a unifying diagnosis (1 mark) 1. 2. 3. 4. Unifying diagnosis"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline your management priorities for this patient. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. The patient develops acute severe kidney injury with oliguria."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List (3) complications you would monitor for in this patient (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."}],"propPages":["asset:imgf4828c0d71455317de"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong>Extensive peri-nephric stranding R&gt;L</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong>Stone in right renal pelvis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong>Proximal right hydroureter</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>4. </strong>Air in right kidney</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Unifying diagnosis : </strong>Emphysematous pyelonephritis. May accept “infected, obstructed</div><div class=\"answer-line\">stone” if presence of gas-forming organisms noted somewhere.<strong> </strong></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong>Haemodynamic resuscitation : judicious crystalloid bolus (10mL/kg) reassess and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">repeat aiming for MAP &gt;65mmHg, early use of vasopressors if persistent/worsening</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hypotension</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong>Broad spectrum antibiotic cover: IV amoxicillin/ampicillin 2g plus gentamicin 5-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">7mg/kg OR Tazocin 4.5g acceptable</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong>Urgent urology referral for surgical decompression of kidney either with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">percutaneous nephrostomy (IR) or JJ stent</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>4. </strong>Analgesia – safe agents, e.g. IV fentanyl 25-50mcg, paracetamol. NSAIDs not ideal.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Will need HDU/ICU-level care due to risks of progressive septic shock, likely needing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">vasopressors/post perc nephrostomy</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>1. </strong>Hyperkalaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>2. </strong>Metformin-related lactic acidosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>3. </strong>Hypoglycaemia due to renal accumulation of gliclazide</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"A 72-year-old fisherman is retrieved from the ocean after being swept off a rock-ledge. He\nhas a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He has been bought directly to your ED\nby road. On arrival to resus, your team applies monitoring and obtains IV access. Initial vitals are as follows :\n\nHR 120, BP 90/ 55, RR 28, Sats 94% on 15L/min O2 via NRB\n\nGCS 13, BSL 3.1, Temp 30°C\nThere is no external evidence of major head trauma, no gross limb deformity and he can move all limbs. He appears to be tiring.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State details of your FIRST (4) steps in management. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Name (2) risk factors for drowning by each category (4 marks) Sociodemographic Underlying medical condition"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"In addition to hypothermia, list (4) complications of drowning. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. <strong>Respiratory support</strong> : commence NIV (CPAP / BiPAP) high PEEP up to 12-15cmH2O</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and high FiO2 titrated to Sats &gt; 94%.  Plan for intubation in event of further</div><div class=\"answer-line\">deterioration.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. <strong>Address haemodynamics</strong> : judicious crystalloid boluses aiming systolic &gt; 100,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">progress to vasopressors if inadequate response aiming MAP &gt;65mmHg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. <strong>Warm patient</strong> :  remove wet clothes, adequately dry, start active external warming,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">give IV ﬂuids through warmer</div><div class=\"answer-line\">4. <strong>Address hypoglycaemia</strong> : 25-50mls 50% dextrose aim BSL &gt; 5, monitor BSL hourly</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Sociodemographic</strong>: inadequately supervised infant / male aged 16-24 / risk taking</div><div class=\"answer-line\">behaviour / drug or ETOH intoxication</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Underlying medical condition:</strong> epilepsy / diabetes / CAD or cardiomyopathy</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Ischaemic cardiomyopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Electrolyte derangement -&gt; arrythmia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• AKI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Multi-organ failure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>(Need bold for full marks otherwise max score 2/4) </strong></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"A 28-year-old man is brought into your tertiary ED having been witnessed to jump from a 2nd storey balcony (approximately 9 metres) landing onto a concrete driveway below. There was no report of headstrike, however, he was found confused, agitated and complaining of back pain. On arrival his vitals are as follows :\n\nHR\n130\n\nBP\n85/65\n\nRR\n\nSats\n95% on 15L/min NRB\n\nGCS\n\nTemp\n35.7°C","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Give (4) ultrasound findings that can help determine the cause of hypotension in this patient. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (4) elements of haemostatic resuscitation. (4 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. The patient undergoes CT panscan, a slice of which is shown below :"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What is your interpretation? (1 mark)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List (3) possible management strategies for this CT finding, providing the clinical indication for each. (6 marks) Management Strategy Clinical Indication"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Give (3) advantages of TEG/ROTEM in managing the shocked trauma patient. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."}],"propPages":["asset:imgccee0289f276c068fb"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Intraperitoneal ﬂuid / positive FAST – intrabdominal haemorrhage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Spine sign – massive haemothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Absent lung slide / barcode sign / lung point – tension pneumothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Pericardial eﬀusion + R-heart collapse – pericardial tamponade</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Flat IVC + hyperdynamic LV – blood loss</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Limitation of crystalloid use, early use of blood products in 1: 1: 1or 2:1:1 ratio</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Maintenance of circulating blood volume - accept lower than normal MAP as long as</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cerebral perfusion maintained and no TBI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Limitation of ongoing bleeding eg. tourniquet, packing</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Use of antiﬁbrinolytic agents ie. TXA</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• POC coagulation testing ie. TEG/ROTEM or track ﬁbrinogen/INR/platelets</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Correction/prevention of hypothermia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Calcium replacement</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypoattenuation &gt;50% visualised right lobe of liver consistent with traumatic injury with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">perihepatic haematoma consistent with AAST grade IV injury (high grade)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>Conservative </em></td><td><em>Stable haemodynamics/rapid response to volume resuscitation </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>and immediate availability of surgical/interventional means of </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>control if deterioration (and no evidence of hollow viscus injury) </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Interventional</td><td>Haemodynamically unstable/transient response to volume</td></tr><tr><td>radiology/angiographic</td><td>resuscitation, resource availability, amendable injury identified</td></tr><tr><td>embolisation</td><td>on CT angio</td></tr><tr><td>Surgical</td><td>Haemodynamically unstable and poorly responsive to volume</td></tr><tr><td>resuscitation, resource availability (no lesser invasive means</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>available)</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Rapid, speciﬁc identiﬁcation of coagulation abnormalities eg. hypocoagulability, clot</div><div class=\"answer-line\">stability, hyperﬁbrinolysis seen in trauma-induced coagulopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Guide targeted blood-product administration (better management of MTP)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Real-time monitoring of haemostatic resuscitation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Reduction in blood product use -&gt; minimises risks associated with over-transfusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Shorter time to haemostasis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Improved survival</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"A 3-year-old girl presents to your rural ED with 2 days of sore throat, diarrhoea, fevers and increasing work of breathing. She has no significant past medical history, no regular medications and vaccinations are up to date. She appears lethargic and her initial observations are as follows:\n\nRR\n\nSats\n91% RA\n\nHR\n190 CRT 5 seconds\n\nTemp\n38.5 ̊C She undergoes a mobile chest X-ray in resus :","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State the MOST LIKELY diagnosis and 2 causative agents (1/2 mark each). (2 marks) Most likely diagnosis\nCausative agent 1\nCausative agent 2"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List (3) differential diagnoses for the X-ray appearance. (3 marks) 1. 2. 3."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State and justify (6) investigations you would perform in this child. (6 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List (2) indications for chest drain insertion in a child with this clinical presentation. (2 marks) 1. 2."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Outline (5) INITIAL management priorities for this child. (5 marks) 1. 2. 3. 4. 5."}],"propPages":["asset:imgaa1490984c5d1780f9"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Most likely parapneumonic eﬀusion secondary to Strep pneumonia, GAS (Strep</div><div class=\"answer-line\">pyogenes or Staph aureus)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Empyema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Atypical infection (TB, mycoplasma)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Malignant pleural eﬀusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Serositis from rheumatologic cause</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• FB/collapse/consolidation less likely as appearances more in keeping in eﬀusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(tracheal deviation to contralateral side, rib spreading)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bedside</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lung ultrasound - delineate consolidation vs eﬀusion, simple vs loculated eﬀusion for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">drainage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiac ultrasound - exclude pericardial eﬀusion as source of shock</div><div class=\"answer-line\">VBG - deﬁne acid/base status, exclude hypoglycaemia and guide resuscitation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ECG - exclude SVT as cause of tachycardia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Laboratory</div><div class=\"answer-line\">FBC - assess for elevated WCC in sepsis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">EUC - identify concurrent renal failure, electrolyte imbalance from diarrhoea</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Blood cultures - guide further antibiotic therapy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bacterial Throat swab - identify causative organism to guide therapy, esp iGAS (better</div><div class=\"answer-line\">candidates)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Viral respiratory panel - identify viral causes for consideration of disease modifying</div><div class=\"answer-line\">drugs eg inﬂuenza, COVID</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pneumococcal PCR (would this be available in a rural centre?)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CRP (1/2 mark) – to guide disease trajectory</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Radiology - NO MARKS FOR CT (not indicated in this age group or process)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">• Complicated eﬀusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Severe respiratory distress</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Septic shock (drain may be required for source control)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• Ongoing fever and/or respiratory distress after 48 hours of IV antibiotics</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">o High Flow O2 - 2L/kg titrate to SPO2 &gt;94% and improved WOB OR CPAP<strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Intubation and ventilation not appropriate as initial management</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Treat shock  </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o NS 10-20ml/kg bolus repeat q5-10 minutes aim for CRT&lt;3s, max 60ml/kg then</div><div class=\"answer-line\">start vasopressor therapy<strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Early antibiotic therapy </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o Cefotaxime 50mg/kg IV bolus OR<strong> </strong>Ceftriaxone 50mg/kg IV OR<strong> </strong>Ampicillin</div><div class=\"answer-line\">50mg/kg IV AND<strong> </strong>Gentamicin 7mg/kg IV OR<strong> </strong>Clindamycin 15mg/kg IV (iGAS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">coverage)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Supportive </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">o antipyretics eg paracetamol 15mg/kg PO<strong>,  </strong>ibuprofen 10mg/kg PO  – caution</div><div class=\"answer-line\">as may have AKI, seek and treat hypoglycaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">• <strong>Disposition : early organisation of retrieval to tertiary paediatric centre </strong></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false}];
